[{:seon.task/name "roots-procs-are-the-first-forks-graph"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: flow. The owner's design, confirmed 2026-11-16 (decisions: the system process is the fork point): the binary's main process is the system, single-threaded for life; it loads main by load-diff! of its held db against each commit notice, collects, and forks. Root is its first fork; agents and test children are root's siblings and inherit only what the system process built once. Bound: a forked agent's own memory <=5 MB, no baggage (docs/cost.md#the-test-systems-lower-bound); every READY gives a fresh agent's private bytes after fork, after a collection, after one read, beside 5 MB. Flow: root's procs as the first fork's graph, started in root, none in the system process; the commit stream to the system process as one ordered Flow path, a drain answered. Flow's answer to runtime was sent 2026-11-16 (the fork point single-threaded, the writer process holding every proc). SPLIT (agreed): the first-notice load in seon.fork-server/serve is runtime's, with flow's proof seon.procs-test/a-bare-fork-server-takes-main-from-its-first-notice; one collection per notice that moved the program, after the load, never before a fork; test children forked by the system process at main's program, loading their branch's diff (tests2, seon.prove/tests-fork-from-the-branchs-warm-agent). NAMES (owner): the system process is the binary's main, the fork server and every agent's parent; the writer process is its first fork (today's root: the writer, the api, the reconciler). THE STOP (deps, in seon.main/the-binary-main-is-the-system-process): the writer process ending with :stop or :hand-over ends the system process's group and the process; a crash of the writer process or its restart on the same binary leaves the system process and its agents running and forks a new writer process. FLOW'S NEXT ITEM: the agent's :transport proc (seon.repl/transport-step) reconnects instead of ending on the writer process's EOF: each wait on it answered typed :seon.system/restarting, then a reconnect to the writer process through the system process's listener (:seon.server/root-port in the agent's start map, deps) and the waits re-asked; the new writer process adopting an agent it did not spawn is runtime's side, to agree. MEASURE (deps, corrected): an agent's own memory is its private dirty bytes (vmmap SM=PRV dirty; jolt.host/process-private-bytes, read inside the process), never phys_footprint, which also counts dirty pages still shared copy-on-write with the parent (deps: a fresh agent's 225 MB footprint = 52.1 MB its own + 172.9 MB shared; flow's 626 / 646 / 652 MB footprints overstate the same way). The 5 MB row is held to private bytes, read in one eval after the fork, after (System/gc), after one read. STATE 2026-11-16 (flow, at compaction): ON MAIN: the hand-over op, L2 step 1 (restarting), the stop fact inside the drain, the stall detector deleted (the drain has no bound now), B1 (:call frame, :write names no branch), G2 (seon.agent/*start*), :seon.task/cause deleted. ON jolt/flow, unmerged: docs/flow.md 'Every proc is seen' and 'Every process is a Flow process'. NEXT, in order: (1) web's Flow view (seon.web/the-system-seen-from-flow, web builds, flow reviews): flow's parts are two agent transport ops, {:op :flow-graph} (datafy of its graph) and {:op :flow-ping} (one ping), and the graph's report and error chans as a mult the api's :back and web tap; deps asked for two Jolt changes (a transition and a parked put announced on the report chan). Upstream flow-monitor is cloned at reference-code/core.async.flow-monitor. (2) G1: the transport's :waits into its proc (no stall decision); G3: the :repl fields; G4: a :branch proc; then the atom deleted; B2: the writer process gets the :repl proc, agent=root lands there, seon.server/answer's :root branch deleted. (3) the agent reconnect (deps' :seon.server/root-port). (4) arm-wall! as a test's estimate deadline (with tests2); bin/stalls and bin/evidence. (5) the 17 s drain (not reproduced: the last stop on record, runs/root.log.prev, took 423 ms, drain 70 ms and flow-stop 345 ms; root to name where 17.4 s was read). THE STOP-START PING RECORD (root's yes 2026-11-16): PRIOR WORK: stop! prints jolt.host/fiber-dump at its start (system.clj:2441), proved in its log by seon.system-test/a-root-resumes-a-store-an-earlier-build-wrote; Jolt's flow/ping already answers each proc's status, count, state, ::flow/site and ::flow/in-fill, and ::flow/unanswered and ::flow/report-dropped as metadata, waiting only on procs not mid-step (reference-code/jolt flow/impl.clj:391-434); web's agreed snapshot read (seon.web/the-system-seen-from-flow, interface (2)) is that answer; searched src for flow/ping and seon.graph for a ping, snapshot or status function: none. DESIGN: seon.graph/snapshot, the snapshot read web's view and the stop share: Jolt's ping answer with each proc's ::flow/ins and ::flow/outs left out (channel objects, the topology being datafy's), its metadata kept; stop! prints it beside the fiber dump. COST: n = 79 procs on root [M]; per proc one control put and one pong, ~900 ticks -> ~71k ticks, 25 KB printed. MEASURED at root's REPL: 4.1-7.0 ms, 71k-84k ticks, 24,775 chars [result/cf3b1fd2, result/ad8995ba]. RISK, named to root: ping's timeout (1000 ms) is a clock on our own procs; it bounds the stop's start only when an idle proc fails to answer, and that proc is then named under ::flow/unanswered. LESSONS: search the codebase and reference-code before building (flow-view duplicated web's task); run bin/laws on the merged tip and bin/build seon before a READY that touches system.edn, schemas or deletes a namespace; a deleted schema key's cites in tasks break the seed; never hold-start! at root's REPL; no long foreground evals (short timeouts). SEON.AGENT/STATE INTO PROCS (flow's, from runtime by agreement 2026-11-16; root approved the order): its 45 fields [M, a live agent] in four groups, each landed red-first: G1 the :transport proc's (:waits, :stall-told: the transport the socket's one owner both ways, an ask registered and sent in one step); G2 the start map (id key task branch cfg mode store-conn flow serve ns, inherited-bytes env-mismatch: fixed at start, handed to the procs; runtime's test-child start and config ruling land after it); G3 the :repl proc's (eval n repl-vars busy interrupt-ok walk walk-whole? parts-walk marks turn sent-turn inbox load-errors uncontracted fn-costs results; my.test/eval-in an ask of it); G4 a :branch proc, its branch's one writer (db base main merged-t tx-seq branch-changes main-changes base-graph index index-builds schema-registry program misaligned no-merge-base unwatched). Then the atom is deleted. FLOW'S OTHER CENSUS ITEM (docs/flow.md#every-process-is-a-flow-process): seon.main's stdin thread (an io-thread reading stdin for its EOF) goes when the system process owns start and stop: the writer process's end is a message from it, never a read of its stdin."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-throwing-step-holds-its-message-and-parks",
             :plan
             "Reproduction (e3_failure E3.1): a throwing step loses its message (flow/impl.clj proc catch), keeps its old state and goes on; the only record is a sliding(100) error chan. With the hold pattern (e3 `held`, ~25 lines) the proc keeps the message, emits a :failed fact with the cause, and narrows ::flow/input-filter to nothing, so its producers park by back-pressure and its consumers park on empty inputs (E3.2: no crash, no timeout, nothing dropped). On the load effect's one broadcast after a reload (a ::reloaded signal, flow's own signal-select) it retries the held message through the Var; E3.3: the fix ran the held message and the 8 queued behind it, in order, with no other intervention. Correct: seon.step/held (or the same in flow's proc loop), used by every root proc; my.fx's load owner casts ::reloaded with the vars it changed. A request a held proc took is answered when it completes, never with a fake fallback (root's api does this for a throwing step: docs/flow.md).",
             :kind :work,
             :proofs
             #{seon.step-test/a-reload-retries-the-held-message
               seon.step-test/a-throw-holds-the-message-and-records-the-cause}}
